From c4739017e7e5f83884aac39249bb14ef768f3b1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Hardin <kushastronaut@icloud.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 11:41:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Netplay speed: link lookahead, cheaper slice checks,
 per-machine CPU discount

- GbaNetplayLaunch::link_lookahead (opt-in, 0 = off as before): while every
  cable port is in multiplayer mode a native slice may run up to that many
  cycles, kept under one transfer at the current baud rate
  (GbaLinkHub::safe_lookahead), instead of 63. Folded into the identity.
  Duke Nukem Advance (115200 baud) uses 2048: slice switches per frame fell
  from about 25000 to about 6000.
- multiplayer_slice_safe: ARM fast path from the opcode bits (branches,
  data processing, multiplies, immediate-offset LDR/STR) before the decoded
  path; same answers, no hash lookup per instruction.
- Native RAM templates: masked templates compare their fixed runs with
  memcmp and only partly masked bytes one by one (same answer).
- The CPU time discount (g_runtime_cycle_discount_shift and its remainder)
  is part of RuntimeTimingContext and of each machine's saved session state
  (state version 3), and runtime_tick honours it in session execution, so a
  game hook that discounts a pass charges the same time on every peer.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
